   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] structured model definition",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "with both alpha and beta unknown, you will have problems with the term\r<br>\r<br>\talpha * exp(beta)\r<br>\r<br>with \r<br>\r<br>\ty = y1 + y2\r<br>\r<br>and y1 is poisson data, then you&#39;re outside the scope of the R-INLA can\r<br>do.\r<br>\r<br>H\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Mon, 2019-07-15 at 08:53 -0700, Al T. wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hello,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I&#39;m learning r-inla syntax. It is possible to infer the parameters\r<br>&gt; alpha, beta, and gamma for the model defined below? Thanks!\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; t&lt;-1:100\r<br>&gt; alpha&lt;-1\r<br>&gt; beta&lt;-2\r<br>&gt; gamma&lt;-0.001\r<br>&gt; delta&lt;-0.7\r<br>&gt; y1&lt;-rpois(length(t), lambda = alpha * exp(beta+gamma*t))\r<br>&gt; y2&lt;-arima.sim(list(ar=delta), n = length(t)-1)\r<br>&gt; y&lt;-y1+y2 + rnorm(length(t))\r<br>&gt; data&lt;-data.frame(t,y)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I was trying something along the lines of\r<br>&gt; fit = inla(y ~ f(t, model = &#39;ar1&#39;) + f(t.copy, model = &#39;exponential&#39;,\r<br>&gt; copy=T),\r<br>&gt;             data=data,\r<br>&gt;             family = &quot;gaussian&quot;,\r<br>&gt;             control.link=list(model=&quot;identity&quot;))\r<br>&gt; but I&#39;m not sure if that makes sense at all.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
